  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a1/Avengers_Vol_1_4.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The action picks up at the end of the previous issue, where we learn where Namor the Sub-Mariner went after his titanic battle with the Avengers.&nbsp; Namor searches the oceans' depths in vain, searching for his people who abandoned him at the end of last summer's [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1|2026-11-16].\n\n\"Hours later,\" he finds himself \"atop an ice flow in the North Sea\" where he sees a tribe of Eskimos worshipping a \"petrified frozen figure within a cake of ice.\"&nbsp; Mad as he is, he takes them as \"fools\" and commands them to be gone, calling them \"weak, helpless mortals,\" and even tosses their \"idol\" into the sea.&nbsp; It then floats into the warmer waters of the Gulf Stream and begins to melt...\n\n\"Naught remains but a frozen, petrified figure in a state of suspended animation,\" which drifts past the Avengers craft (apparently returning to the US from Gibraltar, the site of last issue's battle).&nbsp; Giant-Man reaches out and pulls it in.&nbsp; They see his colorful costume and shield, and Wasp recognizes it as Captain America!&nbsp; Not only that, but he's <i>alive</i>!\n\nSuddenly, the thawing figure awakens and calls out for \"Bucky!\"&nbsp; He indicates that <i>someone</i> \"can't kill Bucky!\" and that he'll \"smash you <i>all</i>!\"&nbsp; It takes both Thor and Iron Man to hold him still enough for him to finally realize that it's useless.&nbsp; His old partner is dead.&nbsp; Nothing can ever change that!\n\nFinally noticing the garb of those around him, he asks how he got here.&nbsp; Iron Man wants to know the same thing.&nbsp; As the figure dons his mask and picks up his shield, he declares that \"I was once the man the world called -- Captain America!\"&nbsp; The Avengers test him in combat to see if he has the skills, and he does.&nbsp; But they still demand an explanation as to why he hasn't aged since the second World War.\n\nCap then recounts what he remembers about Bucky's tragic death and being thrown into waters \"off the coast of Newfoundland\" and frozen, which he imagines is what kept him from aging.&nbsp; The Avengers accept his explanation.\n\nWhen the Avengers return to NYC and dock their craft, one of the flashes from the photographers' many cameras turns the Avengers to stone!&nbsp; Cap comes up the hatch later and finds the stone figures (mistaking them for statues).&nbsp; He begins to walk around and take in the new scenery.&nbsp; An officer helps him into a hotel where he contemplates his future, but Rick Jones has been following him.&nbsp; Cap is astonished and asserts that Rick Jones could be Bucky's \"double!\"&nbsp; Rick explains that the Avengers are missing, and Cap agrees to help.\n\nCap has Rick bring him pictures of the events at the dock, and sure enough, a \"photographer\" is seen holding a gun.&nbsp; Rick calls on the Teen Brigade to help find this \"photographer.\"&nbsp; Many leads are identified as false before Cap stumbles upon the <i>real</i> one!&nbsp; Cap is sure the gun has come from someplace <i>other</i> than Earth, and he rips off the rubbery mask of the man to reveal what looks like.... an Asparagus Man!\n\nThe alien claims he's from a \"far distant galaxy\" and that he crashed on Earth \"centuries ago.\"&nbsp; He used his gun only to defend himself, and it turned people to stone for one hundred hours.&nbsp; Cap realizes that in the shadows, the creature's hair made him look like a woman, and this must have been the origin of the legend of Medusa!\n\nHe reveals that he encountered the Sub-Mariner, who told him he would free his ship from the depths of the oceans if he would turn the Avengers to stone, and he was coerced to try!&nbsp; Cap remembers the name of Sub-Mariner \"from the dim past!\"&nbsp; He returns the creature to the stone statues, and he restores them after having been promised that the Avengers would try and free the ship themselves!\n\nFrom beneath the sea, the Sub-Mariner sees that his attempt has failed by \"the one who calls himself Captain America\" (his memory too has still not fully recovered it seems).&nbsp; Sub-Mariner realizes that he can't rely on anyone to help him, and he even runs into a troop of his \"elite guard\" who bow in acknowledgement -- he is no longer alone after all!\n\nThe next day has the Avengers working alongside the alien to help free his ship, Thor's hammer providing the finishing touches.&nbsp; But suddenly, the Sub-Mariner and his troops attack!&nbsp; Subby and Iron Man engage, with the Sub-Mariner getting the upper hand and appearing about to smash the armor, but his troops call on him to help them against Thor, who is eager to face off against \"a foe more worthy of my mettle!\"&nbsp; As they engage, Thor even calls him \"Witless mutant!\"&nbsp; Giant-Man gets into the fray also, and Sub-Mariner realizes he cannot lift Thor's hammer, which Cap (watching from a distance) calls the \"most awesome weapon I've ever seen.\"\n\nSub-Mariner still refuses to surrender!&nbsp; And he has Rick Jones as a hostage.&nbsp; This causes Cap to finally jump in and help as well, infuriated over \"the lad\" being in danger.&nbsp; But their battle is interrupted by a \"titanic undersea explosion\" which causes the island to fall to pieces.&nbsp; Sub-Mariner retreats with his troops, sure that the Avengers won't have survived, though he admits \"They were worthy foes!&nbsp; They deserved a more fitting end!\"\n\nBut it's not an undersea earthquake but rather the alien creature's now-freed and repaired spaceship launching from the depths.&nbsp; It's ironic that the alien who had previously tried to <i>destroy</i> the Avengers ends up unwittingly rescuing them from further attacks by Namor and his elite guard.&nbsp; By the time the Sub-Mariner learns that the Avengers still live, it's far too late.&nbsp; As the Avengers gather themselves, we find that the Wasp was \"doing what <i>any</i> girl would do in a moment of crises [sic] - powdering my nose, of course!\"\n\nCap's courage and fighting skills earn him an invitation to join the Avengers!&nbsp; Meanwhile, Rick wonders how him teaming up with Captain America will affect his relationship with the Hulk.",
  "grade_one_liner": "The story asks readers to overlook an awful lot, yet somehow still succeeds because the moment itself is bigger than the mistakes surrounding it.",
  "subtitle": "Captain America Covers a Multitude of Sins",
  "review_text": "Even someone living in the 1960s <i>had</i> to have sensed the importance of this issue, which featured an [SPAN: severity=info; text=absolutely legendary comic character] appearing after a 20 year absence.&nbsp; Teen readers who might have been unfamiliar with Marvel's publication history only needed to take note of the reactions of the various Avengers in the early going -- that would be evidence enough to understand that [SPAN: severity=success; text=something extraordinary was occurring before their eyes,] which likely would have elicited a thrill in its own right.&nbsp; And given the preponderance of letters from older readers who <i>did</i> remember -- to <i>them</i>, it must have seemed like Christmas morning!\n\n[ASSET: left | SplashPageSpoiler | width=12%] Indeed, the splash page <i>alone</i> announces a \"magnificent milestone\" and indicates how the return of Captain America was significant to both Stan Lee <i>and</i> Jack Kirby, because of events that had transpired in the \"Golden Age of Comics.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] And let's face it, this page also [SPAN: severity=warning; text=spoils the ending,] coming right out and saying that [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Captain America joins the Avengers!\"]&nbsp; (But I don't imagine anyone actually cared, so I won't penalize the story for tipping readers off.)\n\nMarvel expertly uses Namor's flight from the Avengers at the end of last issue to land him in the North, and the first couple of pages set the stage for how the frozen block of ice containing the body of the legendary World War II hero drifts downstream and finally into the path of the Avengers' craft.\n\nAs we know that it was \"hours later\" (page 2) when Namor encountered the Eskimos before he even tossed the \"petrified figure\" into warmer waters, we may fairly ask [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what the Avengers were <i>doing</i> during all that time and why it took them so long to return to the United States from Gibraltar] (which you might remember was the site of last issue's battle).&nbsp; And how did the icy figure manage to float all the way down to what is roughly the same latitude as the state of North Carolina?\n\nNevertheless, I'm sure most readers are willing to overlook such nitpicky questions and instead revel in the presence of the man who Wasp <i>immediately</i> recognizes as <i>Captain America</i>!&nbsp; Even <i>Thor</i> indicates that he's heard of him!\n[ASSET: center | ItsCaptainAmerica | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: right | OnceCaptainAmerica | width=13%] Yes, this Captain America is famous, and he does provide at least <i>some detail</i> as to the events that eventually led him here, but from a storytelling perspective, [SPAN: severity=error; text=there are <i>definitely</i> some holes in his story,] not the least of which is that he seems to instantly know that a lot of time has passed since his most recent memories.\n[BR]\n[BR] He even puts on the rest of his costume and says:\n[BR] \"I was once the man the world called -- Captain America!\"\n\nWhy would he phrase it in such a manner?&nbsp; To him, [SPAN: severity=info; text=no time at all has passed,] so he is <b><i>still</i></b> Captain America, not \"once I <b>was</b>...\"&nbsp; At this point in the story, no one has yet told him that World War II is over.&nbsp; That doesn't happen until the <i>next page</i>, where the Avengers finally wonder how he could be the <i>real</i> Captain America since he's obviously still young.\n\n[ASSET: left | BuckyDropOff | width=12%] [ASSET: right | SuspendedAnimation | width=12%] When Cap finally details the last events he remembers, he says that \"It seems like only yesterday -- but it was more than twenty years ago...\"\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>True</i>, the context of their speech does serve to inform him that <i>some amount of time</i> has gone by, but [SPAN: severity=warning; text=how does he know it's been 20 years?]\n\nThe sad story of Bucky's death as a result of the explosion is heart-breaking, of course, but it directly leads to Captain America being thrown into a state of suspended animation.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: All the while, portrayed in the background is a dastardly figure later revealed as Baron Zemo!]\n\nHe even knows somehow that he dropped into the waters \"off the coast of Newfoundland\" (which I must say is <i>quite the distance</i> from Europe, especially after the same page 7 indicates he was at an army base in the \"European Theater of Operations\").&nbsp; He even knows that 1) he was frozen, and 2) later found by Eskimos.\n\nI'm sorry, but I <b>gotta ask</b>:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=how can you know you were found by Eskimos <i>after</i> you were frozen?]&nbsp; You can <i>perhaps</i> explain away how you might have overheard some discussion between the Avengers about the elapsed time before you fully woke up, but <i>how can you explain the Eskimos</i> that even the <i>Avengers</i> never encountered?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=There's just <i>no way</i>!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(As I said... some <i>serious holes</i> in the tale...)]\n\nI must stress this again:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text= We as readers are largely happy to forgive such relatively minor consistency problems and be content that Captain America is both here and now!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | TurnedToStone | width=21%] [ASSET: right | RickJones | width=9%] To no one's surprise, Stan and Jack find a way to feature their new toy in solo action by using the plot device of turning the Avengers to stone so that the only character left is the newly revived Captain America.\n[BR]\n[BR] This serves two purposes, first of which is to give Cap a chance to see the sights of NYC without guides, allowing him to see with his own eyes how much the world has changed since WWII -- fashions, cars, skyscrapers, television, etc.\n\nSecondly, it gives Cap a chance to meet Rick Jones, who conveniently follows him to the hotel room and demands he help track down the missing Avengers, or else he'll mention Cap's name to his friend, the Hulk.&nbsp; (Cap indicates <i>correctly</i> that he has no idea who this \"Hulk\" is.)&nbsp; Also, very conveniently, Rick looks <i>exactly</i> like his old partner Bucky, Cap even pointing out that [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"You could be Bucky's <i>double</i>!\"]\n\nRick Jones' presence in NYC is again a problem with consistency, as he was clearly in New Mexico in the last issue.&nbsp; <i>Granted</i>, we know that the Hulk was in the water for \"days\" after that, before he finally ran into the Sub-Mariner, so Rick certainly has had enough time to reach NYC, but <b><i>why</i></b>?\n\nIt's kind of jarring to keep finding him popping up anywhere the story demands he be.&nbsp; (I'll get back to this later.)\n\n[ASSET: left | DimPast | width=12%] [ASSET: right | BackInMyDay | width=16%] Cap eventually tracks down the alien (who looks like he might be some kind of \"Asparagus Man\"), frees the Avengers, and the rest of the book involves this alien and the Sub-Mariner, with, by far, the most interesting thing being that [SPAN: severity=info; text=both Captain America <i>and</i> the Sub-Mariner seem to be largely unaware of each other] (despite the fact that the old comics had them appearing as allies during World War II).\n\nAnother story inconsistency is that page 14 of this issue indicates that the Sub-Mariner contacted the Asparagus Man \"some days ago,\" promising to help him free his ship if he were to arrange to turn the Avengers to stone.&nbsp; Yet page 18 of <i>last </i>issue's story indicates that Namor and the Hulk went to Gibraltar only \"hours\" after their first encounter with each other.&nbsp; And then, early in <i>this</i> issue, it's only \"hours later\" (after the end of [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]) that Namor shows up and interrupts the Eskimos.\n\nSo... [SPAN: severity=error; text=how could he have known to contact the alien \"some days ago\" -- before he ever decided with the Hulk to even <i>attack</i> them?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And let's not even <i>discuss</i> how Namor even knew of this alien in the <i>first place</i> and what ability he had!&nbsp; We won't <i>go</i> there!)]\n\nPages 17-22 are essentially one large battle, with the Sub-Mariner facing off first against Iron Man and then Thor (with the Wasp largely missing in action after she buzzes around Namor's head at the bottom of page 18).\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=As always, Jack's rendition of Thor is a spectacle to behold!]\n[ASSET: center | ThorIsUnstoppable | width=67%]\n(Even in a story dominated by Captain America's return, Kirby somehow still manages to make Thor look like the biggest force on the page.)\n\nReturning to the matter of Rick Jones, his random appearance in NYC wasn't even the <i>worst</i> of the problems.\n\nAfter being completely absent from the book's action since page 11 (though he seems to be in the background without comment on both pages 15 and 16), he's somehow [SPAN: severity=warning; text=conjured up and pulled out of the proverbial hat] by none other than the Sub-Mariner as a hostage on the book's second-to-last page.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=This makes no sense!]&nbsp; How would Sub-Mariner have even <i>known</i> about him?&nbsp; This bridge too far moment is particularly damning and <i>definitely</i> plays a role in the assigned grade.\n\nOnly the sudden launch of the alien's ship out into space fools Namor into believing the battle is won, and that the Avengers are finished.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(They <i>aren't</i>, of course -- the book has only run out of <i>pages</i>!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | WelcomeToTheAvengers | width=12%] That only left the matter of officially welcoming Captain America to the Avengers and leaving Rick to ponder the Hulk's whereabouts and his future as his partner, indicating that:\n[BR]\n[BR] \"Captain America has replaced him.\"\n[BR] What will <i>happen</i> to the Hulk now?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Will his mug finally be removed from the book's upper-left corner logo?)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nDespite the <i>plethora</i> of consistency problems, there's [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=no question] that this story was ambitious (to say the <i>least</i>).&nbsp; In fact, it's probably fair to say that Stan and Jack tried to pack <b>too many things</b> into the story.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=But that doesn't change the fact that it is clearly ground-breaking, which in this case, causes a reader to overlook many of the story's ills.]\n\nThis issue absolutely <i>demands</i> that we remember what the intent was.&nbsp; It was stated on the splash page, and the story never shied away from it, even as the alien, the timing issues, Cap's seemingly-magical ability to recall the Eskimos, Rick Jones' resemblance to Bucky, his knack for appearing wherever the plot requires him, the Sub-Mariner's sudden vendetta against the Avengers, and countless other oddities piled up around it...\n\nEven though these inconsistencies were numerous and <i>yes</i>, did end up costing the story points in the end, the <b>ambition</b> in this particular case far outweighs the other, more ordinary factors of storytelling.\n\n[HANG0] At the end of the day, is a reader more likely to remember the issue's host of problems?\n[HANG0] Or is that reader likelier to remember the bold cover and ambitious turn of events that brought Captain America into the Silver Age?\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=<i>Surely</i>, it is the latter!]\n[BR] (And that is why the story still lands on the upper end of the scale.)\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=It's no masterpiece -- that is for <i>certain</i>] -- but [SPAN: severity=success; text=it succeeds in all the right ways] and brings back a beloved character from the Golden Age of Comics, exactly as the splash page promises!\n\nIts <b>importance</b> is clear right up front, even if you were being introduced to the character for the first time.\n[BR] So, on the whole, I say [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=job well done] to Stan and Jack.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=It took a lot of guts to pull this off!]",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: left | DifferentAvengers4Cover | width=15%] I should point out that in the other comic mags of the month, on the pages that advertise this issue on sale (e.g. [LINK:1964:ASM_11|ASM #11|2027-05-07]), most, but not <i>all</i> of them (e.g. <i>Sgt. Fury</i> #6) have a shot of the cover, but they include the text \"THE REAL\" in a box above \"Captain America Lives Again!\" - that is <i>not</i> on the <i>actual</i> front cover.&nbsp; I suppose after the Cap tryout in [LINK:1963:ST_114_torch1|ST #114torch|2026-12-28], Marvel wanted readers to know it was the <b>real</b> one this time.&nbsp; The word \"SENSATIONAL\" is also highlighted differently.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Note that I've highlighted the differences in yellow...)]\n\n[HANG0] * As I pointed out in last issue's review ([LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]), Jack Kirby's insistence on drawing Iron Man flying with his [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=knees bent] (and in this case, <i>right on the front cover</i>) is a continuing source of irritation for me.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=No other artist draws him this way,] but Kirby really does seem to believe that such a thing is possible.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\n[HANG0] * It's interesting that even the Eskimos have heard of Namor, page 3 showing one of them saying, \"It is the dread <i>Namor</i> -- the legendary one!\"&nbsp; However, it's important to note that the Sub-Mariner isn't new to the world, having first appeared decades earlier.&nbsp; They would have had years to have heard of this \"legendary one.\"\n\n[HANG0] * The book has a full-page ad featuring the cover of [LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05] and indicates that it's \"NOW ON SALE!\"&nbsp; While that still may be true in most places, it is curious that it is <i>last month's</i> issue, not <i>this</i> month's [LINK:1964:ASM_11|ASM #11|2027-05-07] (which would be on sale in just a week).&nbsp; Not worth more than a passing head-scratch..."
  ],
  "ownership": "I was fortunate enough to own two copies of this issue, both of which were part of the 2000 collection.\n\nThe very ragged copy (with tears in the cover), in fact, was the very first one I sold out of my collection when I started listing them on eBay, ultimately going for $227.50 in Feb 2019, netting me almost $116.\n\nI pressed the nicer one later that month and took it to <i>Comics to Astonish</i> to have it submitted to CGC, but before the owner actually got around to shipping it, a customer came in, wanting that <i>exact</i> book, and he called me and offered me $1200 on the spot for it (still in its \"raw\" form).&nbsp; I decided to take the money, as that was a fair price at the time (and saved me the inevitable eBay fees).&nbsp; I ended up netting over $1100 on that book, which later paid half the price of a new refrigerator.&nbsp; (Another comic I sold to him paid for the <i>other</i> half, which you'll hear about later.)\n\nSo the net result was most of the funds needed to buy a new refrigerator in my home in early 2019 came from two comic books!",
  "decoder_ring": "[ASSET: left | AsparagusMan | width=18%] I have no idea if John Byrne used this \"Asparagus Man\" appearance as the basis of how he depicted the doomed D'bari in that now-famous issue of the <i>X-Men</i> years later (XMEN #135).&nbsp; But as one who read <i>that</i> issue first and then <i>later</i> read this one, the resemblance is... <i>well</i>... \"uncanny.\"&nbsp; And yes, Dark Phoenix <i>is</i> depicted as travelling to a far, distant galaxy in that issue, so there is no inconsistency on <i>that</i> score at least.",
  "highlight": "The Sub-Mariner, Rick Jones, a stranded alien, and the Avengers all play second fiddle to Captain America's legendary return."
}